WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.046 --> 00:00:02.100
(uneasy music)

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.300
- [Narrator] Russian tanks that cost

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.880
over a million dollars are being taken out

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.343
by cheap consumer-style drones,

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.393
like this one that cost
a few hundred dollars.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.580
Since the Ukrainian
counteroffensive began,

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.210
military analysts say there's
been a dramatic increase

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.280
in Ukraine's use of FPV,

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.440
or first person view drones

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.200
with most Ukrainian
battalions using these drones

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.590
to execute kamikaze-style
attacks on Russian tanks,

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.893
troop positions, and
other large scale weapons.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.100
The aim is twofold:

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.290
to make the military
less dependent on weapons

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:44.897
from western countries
and to operate cheaply.

00:00:44.897 --> 00:00:47.564
(drone buzzing)

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.990
The Wall Street Journal
collected dozens of attack videos

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.850
from Ukrainian drone teams

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.430
and spoke to analysts
from Janes Intelligence

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.893
to break down how these
attacks were executed.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.860
Kamikaze drones must get
very close to a target

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.723
and remain undetectable.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.130
To execute an attack like
this, FPV drone pilots

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.440
work in teams to identify targets

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.213
and get into position to strike.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.700
The team usually includes a driver

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.120
to get to the attack location
and two drone pilots,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.460
one for the attack or kamikaze drone,

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.210
and one for a reconnaissance drone.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.380
The optimal flight distance for missions

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.693
is roughly one to two miles to the target,

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.173
but they can launch from
as far as seven miles.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.230
One way they evade detection

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:40.650
is by using low quality cameras on FPVs.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.540
The signals these cameras transmit

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.130
are less likely to be scrambled

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.623
by Russian electronic warfare systems.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.810
Before launching, the
operators attach explosives

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.483
to the kamikaze drone
with tape and hardware.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.240
The attack drone operator wears

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.827
a virtual reality style
headset to control the drone.

00:02:00.827 --> 00:02:03.494
(drone buzzing)

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.330
After launching, a reconnaissance drone

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.770
looks for the target.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.260
In this footage from
the Zaporizhzhia region

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.163
near the city of Orikhiv,
it's a Russian truck.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:16.950
Ukrainians targeted it

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:20.610
because it was carrying
ammunition to Russian troops.

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.430
The attack drone follows
the truck as it turns

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.043
and crashes into it
detonating its payload.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.820
When striking a supply truck like this,

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.823
a drone pilot will aim for the
cabin to take out the driver.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.770
The strategy is similar
when targeting tanks.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.510
The most vulnerable spot on a tank

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:43.173
is its engine compartment
located here on the rear side.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.250
Drone pilots often aim for them,

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.203
as in this attack from behind.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.640
The drones strike the
weakest points on tanks

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.060
and heavy machinery like these
infantry fighting vehicles.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.580
Here, the open hatch is a vulnerability

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.383
the pilot likely noticed.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.050
The pilot appears to hit the turret

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.030
where some of the vehicles crew sit

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:08.250
and ammunition is stowed.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.830
Here a kamikaze drone
strikes a Russian truck

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:12.510
that lays landmines.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.363
On these trucks pilots
target the mine modules.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.180
Deterring these trucks is key,

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.340
as minefields have been a major obstacle

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.863
in Ukraine's fight to regain territory.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.010
The failure rate of missions can be high,

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.890
according to Ukrainian units we spoke to.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.660
Russian firepower can knock these drones

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.820
out of the sky if discovered,

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:34.713
as shown by this near miss.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:40.500
About 100 Ukrainian
companies manufacture FPVs,

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.140
40 of which receive government contracts.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.170
Most of the parts these
companies use come from China

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.273
and are becoming difficult to source.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.800
Ukraine's use of these DIY style weapons

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.800
is rapidly evolving, and
now Russia is catching up.

00:03:58.498 --> 00:04:01.165
(drone buzzing)

